WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.450
BioMuse: benchmarking LLMs for trustworthy retrieval in computational biology, using structured Zotero graphs.

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:08.650
A researcher's Zotero library records intent: collections, tags, notes, and links between related papers.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:14.250
BioMuse parses the export into a graph: papers are nodes; edges join papers in the same collection, with shared tags, or linked as related.

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.750
Step 2: tasks are generated from the graph, so the library itself is the answer key.

5
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.350
Step 3: four systems answer the same tasks: GPT-4, Claude 2.1, a fine-tuned LLaMA 2, and BioMuse-RAG, a small model that retrieves over the graph. BM25 is the classical baseline.

6
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.650
GPT-4 leads all four tasks: 82.4% top-3 retrieval and 89% hallucination-free summaries.

7
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.150
But accuracy is not trust: Claude 2.1 is second on retrieval, yet only 73% of its summaries are hallucination-free, below the small BioMuse-RAG at 86.2%.

8
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.550
A fine-tuned LLaMA 2 retrieves no better than keyword search: 51.2% against BM25's 58.2%, p = 0.12.

9
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.850
Now strip the metadata away. Without related items, then without tags, then with titles alone, every model loses accuracy.

10
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.850
GPT-4 falls from 82.4% to 69.5% without related items and tags (p = 0.03); with titles alone, every model ends near or below BM25.

11
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:51.150
Evaluate LLMs against the researcher's own structure, report trust separately from accuracy, and pass the metadata in.
